      "side": 1,
      "message": "Unconditionally dropping all ARPOP_REPLY and unicast-tha ARPOP_REQUEST frames in L2 broadcast when drop_unicast_in_l2_multicast is enabled will break legitimate Gratuitous ARP (GARP) and RFC 5227 Address Announcements:\n\n1. GARP Reply (ARPOP_REPLY with sip \u003d\u003d tip): Legitimate GARP replies (e.g., during router VRRP/HSRP failover) are sent as L2 broadcast (PACKET_BROADCAST) with tha \u003d\u003d sha (see arp_is_garp() in this file, which explicitly requires !memcmp(tha, sha, dev-\u003eaddr_len)). Dropping all ARPOP_REPLY in L2 BMC makes arp_is_garp() unreachable for GARP replies.\n2. GARP Request / ARP Announcement (ARPOP_REQUEST with sip \u003d\u003d tip): Per RFC 5227 §2.1.2, ARP Announcements sent via L2 broadcast may set tha \u003d\u003d sha (the sender\u0027s own unicast MAC) instead of all-zeros.\n3. Separation of sysctls: Linux already provides a separate DROP_GRATUITOUS_ARP sysctl right below (line 798) for networks with ARP proxies (e.g. Hotspot 2.0). DROP_UNICAST_IN_L2_MULTICAST should not implicitly drop legitimate GARP when DROP_GRATUITOUS_ARP is disabled.\n\nSuggested fix: Exempt valid GARP frames (sip \u003d\u003d tip where tha is zero, multicast/broadcast, or equal to sha) so they are deferred to DROP_GRATUITOUS_ARP",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 798,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 798,
        "endChar": 41
      },
      "revId": "be82ae004ce4b00923a57d4ef086c55c992dd048",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
